{"reply":"Alice’s changing size makes identity feel unstable because her body no longer provides a continuous sense of self. In Chapter 2, she treats bodily change as the starting point for the question “Who in the world am I?”; she then tests her memory, knowledge, and voice as if those might reveal whether she is still Alice (Chapter 2, “The Pool of Tears”).

Chapter 5 makes the link explicit. When the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_” Alice answers that she “hardly” knows because she has “been changed several times” and is “so many different sizes in a day” (Chapter 5, “Advice from a Caterpillar”). Size is therefore not just a physical inconvenience: each transformation makes her wonder whether the person inside the changing body is still the same person. At the same time, her uncertain identity intensifies the bodily changes, because she experiences each new size as evidence that she has become someone—or something—different.

So the two elements reinforce each other in a loop: physical change disrupts her identity, and identity doubt makes physical change feel more profound. Carroll also makes that loop circular in the Caterpillar scene: the repeated question “Who are you?” returns Alice to the very puzzle her changing body has created. Up to Chapter 5, does Alice seem to be looking for a fixed definition of herself, or learning that identity may not stay fixed at all?","evidence_uses":[{"supported_claims":["In Chapter 2, she treats bodily change as the starting point for the question “Who in the world am I?”; she then tests her memory, knowledge, and voice as if those might reveal whether she is still Alice (Chapter 2, “The Pool of Tears”)."],"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch02-ln0327-0360","source_location":"Chapter 2 — The Pool of Tears, source lines 327-360","exact_quote":"Who in the world am I?"},{"supported_claims":["When the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_” Alice answers that she “hardly” knows because she has “been changed several times” and is “so many different sizes in a day” (Chapter 5, “Advice from a Caterpillar”).","Size is therefore not just a physical inconvenience: each transformation makes her wonder whether the person inside the changing body is still the same person.","At the same time, her uncertain identity intensifies the bodily changes, because she experiences each new size as evidence that she has become someone—or something—different."],"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln0960-1016","source_location":"Chapter 5 — Advice from a Caterpillar, source lines 960-1016","exact_quote":"“Who are _you?_”"},{"supported_claims":["Chapter 5 makes the link explicit.","Size is therefore not just a physical inconvenience: each transformation makes her wonder whether the person inside the changing body is still the same person.","At the same time, her uncertain identity intensifies the bodily changes, because she experienc