{"reply":"Up to Chapter 5, the text most clearly supports a one-way relationship: Alice’s changing body prompts her to question whether she is still the same person. In Chapter 2, she links the possibility that she has been changed with the question of who she is, then tests her memory, knowledge, and voice for signs of continuity (Chapter 2, “The Pool of Tears”).

Chapter 5 makes that connection more explicit. When the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_” Alice says she hardly knows because she has been “changed several times”; she also describes being “so many different sizes in a day” as confusing (Chapter 5, “Advice from a Caterpillar”). Her altered size and altered memory are presented together, so bodily instability becomes part of the evidence she uses to describe her uncertain identity.

So the two elements reinforce each other thematically: every change in size makes the question of identity harder to settle, while the repeated question “Who are _you?_” directs attention back to those bodily changes. But within the chapters you’ve read, the evidence is stronger for size disrupting identity than for identity uncertainty causing the changes. Is that imbalance part of what makes Alice’s situation feel so disorienting?","evidence_uses":[{"supported_claims":["In Chapter 2, she links the possibility that she has been changed with the question of who she is, then tests her memory, knowledge, and voice for signs of continuity (Chapter 2, “The Pool of Tears”)."],"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch02-ln0327-0360","source_location":"Chapter 2 — The Pool of Tears, source lines 327-360","exact_quote":null},{"supported_claims":["Chapter 5 makes that connection more explicit.","When the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_” Alice says she hardly knows because she has been “changed several times”; she also describes being “so many different sizes in a day” as confusing (Chapter 5, “Advice from a Caterpillar”).","Her altered size and altered memory are presented together, so bodily instability becomes part of the evidence she uses to describe her uncertain identity.","every change in size makes the question of identity harder to settle","the repeated question “Who are _you?_” directs attention back to those bodily changes","But within the chapters you’ve read, the evidence is stronger for size disrupting identity than for identity uncertainty causing the changes."],"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln0960-1016","source_location":"Chapter 5 — Advice from a Caterpillar, source lines 960-1016","exact_quote":"Who are _you?_"}],"memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}

